{
  "completion": "<think>\nOkay, I need to forecast the probability that, by various dates, one of the Millennium Prize Problems (excluding Poincar\u00e9 since it's already solved by 2025) is solved, with AI contributing a key mathematical argument or step. The deadlines are 2026, 2030, 2035, 2040, and 2050.\n\nFirst, I'll list the remaining six problems: Birch and Swinnerton-Dyer, Hodge, Navier-Stokes, P vs NP, Riemann, Yang-Mills. Each has been open for years. The question is whether, by each of these years, at least one is solved with significant AI input.\n\nProbability estimates need to be non-decreasing and between 0 and 1. The problem is whether AI will be capable of contributing substantive proofs. As of 2025, AI has made strides in math research, like assisting in proofs (Apple-grappled problems or certain automatic theorem provers). But Millennium problems are extremely tough; even with human collaboration, solving them is challenging.\n\nBy 2026, only 1 year after the forecast date (2025-04-29), it's extremely early. The chance is very low. Maybe 0.001.\n\nBy 2030, 5 years later, AI could have advanced, but five years is still short. Maybe 0.005.\n\n2035, 10 years later. Maybe AI becomes more collaborative. Perhaps 0.01.\n\n2040: 15 years out. By then, AGI or strong AI might exist. Maybe 0.05.\n\n2050: 25 years later. High chance. Maybe 0.2.\n\nBut I need to consider that AI collaboration could speed up solving, but also that these problems are deep. Some might take longer. The exact roles of AI are unclear. However, since the user wants estimates assuming AI contributes a new argument or proof step, not just computational brute force.\n\nAlso, the event requires correctness being established, which might take time post-publication.\n\nI think the growth in probabilities should be slow but increasing. Assigning 0.001 (2026), 0.01 (2030), 0.05 (2035), 0.2 (2040), 0.5 (2050). Let me check if these are non-decreasing and in 0-1. Yes. But 2030 is 0.01, but normal probabilities. Maybe raise 2030 a bit. Initial thought: 0.001, 0.005, 0.01, 0.05, 0.2, 0.5.\n\nWait, the years are 2026, 2030, 2035, 2040, 2050. So the steps are 1,5,10,15,25 years. So:\n\n2026: 0.001\n\n2030: Maybe 0.001 (four years passed) but perhaps 0.005.\n\n2035: 0.02.\n\n2040: 0.1.\n\n2050: 0.5.\n\nBut this is just a rough order of magnitude. Also, since AI is involved, perhaps higher than human-only. But even so, solving Millennium problems is hard. P vs NP is especially notorious, but others might be more approachable. Hodge conjecture is topological? Maybe AI can brute force examples. Navier-Stokes is physical modeling. AI might find solutions where to restrict the regex. Or perhaps Riemann hypothesis, with number theory, a lot of computational work but proofs might be elusive. But AI's role in generating proofs is speculative.\n\nGiven the time horizons, I think small probabilities for early years, increasing. Maybe:\n\n2026: 0.001\n\n2030: 0.01\n\n2035: 0.05\n\n2040: 0.2\n\n2050: 0.5\n\nThat's my best estimate, given uncertainty. Need to make sure non-decreasing. Yes. And they are all between 0 and 1. Also, even 0.001 is possible that no solution is published with AI contribution by 2026.\n</think>\n\n{\"2026\": 0.001, \"2030\": 0.01, \"2035\": 0.05, \"2040\": 0.2, \"2050\": 0.5}",
